comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-08T13:07:49.463Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation evidence, branch fix/annotation-read-fidelity. runMcpCommentsAction set an implicit producer limit of 20 on every listing. That predates the universal output budget. pm_notes and pm_learnings never had one, and neither does the CLI. Now a listing only sets includeMeta (total_count, has_more) and leaves the bound to the default output budget, whose ordered_suffix retention keeps the newest comments and issues a backward cursor. Keeping the implicit page was rejected: a cursor continuation cannot widen the producer page without changing the row fingerprint the cursor is bound to. Measured on a sandbox item with 30 comments of about 310 characters each through tools/call: page 1 returned indexes 12-30 with has_more true and next_cursor; page 2 returned 1-11 with has_more false and no cursor. Before the change the same walk stopped at index 11 of the 20-row page with has_more true and no cursor. options.limit=N still returns only the newest N, with total_count and has_more. The pm_comments tool description and the contract snapshot were updated, and docs/READ_OUTPUT_CONTRACTS.md records the change. The spec follows next_cursor alone from index 30 to index 1 and asserts has_more equals the presence of a cursor on every page."
  "2026-11-08T14:41:06.001Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of PR 1688. Fixed: the pm_comments tool description and docs/READ_OUTPUT_CONTRACTS.md now say that an explicit options.limit window can report has_more without a cursor, because the caller chose that window and raises or removes the limit to read older comments. outputBudget unbounded returns the complete listing at once, and options.full=true returns the complete history after a write; on a listing, full no longer means anything distinct. The runMcpCommentsAction JSDoc no longer mentions twenty entries. Declined with measurement: the Major about fitting work on very large histories. On a sandbox item with 5,000 comments of about 150 B, the default MCP listing took 1,078 ms, against 833 ms with limit 20 and 765 ms for a 30-comment item; the CLI listing took 713 ms. At 20,000 comments the figures were 1,570 ms default, 1,129 ms with limit 20 and 1,340 ms for a cursor page, against 1,660 ms on the CLI. The cost is linear and dominated by parsing the item document, which a limit does not avoid, and the CLI, pm_notes and pm_learnings already run the same path. A bounded producer cursor for annotation windows is the structural fix, the same design pm-gh1679 owns for item history."
